  than throwing, and a corrupt stored value is treated as absent and overwritten. A login form that breaks because
  storage is blocked is a worse outcome than one that loses correlation. [38;5;153mgetAuth()[39m returning [38;5;153mnull[39m is a normal state that
  callers must handle, not an error.

  [1m4. Constraints going into the spec[0m

  - [1mNothing may gate access or authorization on [38;5;153mIdentity.getAuth()[0m until a server issues it. It is forgeable in
    devtools. Display and correlation only. This is the constraint that stops the placeholder from quietly becoming a
    security control.
  - No password and no token is ever written to either store.
  - The correlation id is a persistent tracker. If any privacy notice exists or is planned, it has to cover it.
  - There is no logout UI today, so [38;5;153mclearAuth()[39m has no caller yet. The spec records that as an open gap the first
    logout-bearing page must close — an auth identity with no teardown path is a half-built feature.

  [1m5. Tooling[0m

  This project has no linter, no formatter, and no test framework, so the cheapest moment to add them is now, before
  [38;5;153midentity.js[39m exists. [38;5;153midentity.js[39m is the first code here with real branching worth testing — storage fallbacks, lazy
  creation, clear semantics. I'd set up unit tests and lint/format, and skip e2e as overkill for this size.

